clove PROTOCOL notes — the I2P BitTorrent dialect and SAM interop
=================================================================

This is a recording of where we pin down things the specs leave vague.
It doubles as the interop lab notebook (SCOPE §9): every place the
upstream spec is underspecified or a library/router behaves surprisingly,
the observation and clove's decision land here. Entries are dated by
milestone, not calendar.

Status legend:
  [decided]   clove behaves this way; verified in-tree or against a router.
  [assumed]   clove behaves this way; NOT yet verified against a live router.
  [open]      unresolved; needs a router or upstream clarification.

Entries keep their number for as long as they are here. A finding that is
superseded rather than obsolete says so in its own tag and points at what
replaced it — being able to see that §2.6a's constraint was removed, and that
the code shaped around it was not revisited for another week, is worth more
than a tidy file. Numbers are not reused, so a gap means an entry that stopped
describing anything clove does.

  1. Addresses and peer identity
  2. SAM and the routers — the session, dialing, and every live surprise
  3. Reconnect discipline
  4. Wire protocol — peer messages, PEX, metadata, and the picker
  5. HTTP tracker announces


1. Addresses and peer identity
------------------------------

1.1 [decided] Peer identity is the 32-byte SHA-256 of a full I2P
    destination (the "destination hash"). This is the only peer identifier
    the engine handles; there is no IP/port anywhere (SCOPE §3).

1.2 [decided] Outbound dial: a peer known only by its 32-byte hash is dialed
    at "<b32>.b32.i2p", where <b32> is RFC 4648 base32 (lowercase, no
    padding) of the 32 hash bytes. 32 bytes -> 52 base32 chars. The router
    resolves the b32 to a full destination via its netDb.
    Implemented in i2pnet::addr, tested against RFC 4648 vectors.

    1.2a [decided] The decode is strict about the final character's spare
      bits. 52 base32 characters carry 260 bits and a destination hash is
      256, so a lax decoder maps sixteen distinct labels onto one identity
      ("…ljnj" and "…ljna" would name the same peer). Found by the mutation
      sweep in i2pnet::addr, and refused: a decoder must not accept what its
      own encoder cannot produce, the same rule as a resume bitfield's
      trailing bits. No interop cost — every implementation derives the label
      as base32(SHA-256(dest)), which always leaves those bits zero — so a
      label with them set was not produced by any conforming client.

1.3 [decided] Inbound / naming: SAM reports a peer's *full* base64
    destination (I2P's base64 alphabet is standard base64 with '+'->'-' and
    '/'->'~'). Its hash is SHA-256 of the decoded destination bytes. These
    compose: base32(SHA-256(dest)) is exactly the b32 label, so a dialed
    peer and an accepted peer reconcile to the same DestHash.

1.4 [decided] Compact tracker peer lists on I2P are concatenated 32-byte
    destination hashes, no port (unlike clearnet's 6-byte ip:port). Non-
    compact responses give full base64 destinations under each peer dict's
    `ip` key. Both parsed in clove-core::tracker.


2. SAM and the routers — the session, dialing, and every live surprise
----------------------------------------------------------------------

2.1 [decided] The SAM host is 127.0.0.1; only the TCP port is configurable.
    This happens to enforce Layer 1's loopback-only rule for free.
    Consequence: a remote SAM bridge is NOT reachable — it would need the
    backend to dial elsewhere. Refused at parse time rather than worked
    around, and there is no escape hatch for it.

    Since 2026-07-29 the configuration says so rather than implying
    otherwise. A remote address and a unix-socket path are both refused at
    parse time, because accepting them meant `cloved -C` approving a
    configuration the runtime then did not honour — a path left the daemon
    with no router, and a remote host was redirected to the same port on
    127.0.0.1, which is a different machine than the one asked for.

2.2 [decided] yosemite's synchronous Stream exposes `split(self) ->
    (ReadHalf, WriteHalf)`, not a duplicating `try_clone`. clove's
    `I2pStream` trait was corrected to a split-based shape (Phase D) so the
    mock and the SAM backend share one honest abstraction, and it matches
    the reader-thread/writer-thread model (Q5) directly.

2.3 [decided, superseded by §2.12] The dial timeout is real: clove's own
    `STREAM CONNECT` bounds the wait for `STREAM STATUS`, which is where a
    leaseSet lookup spends its time, on a socket it owns. Kept because the
    trait's `timeout` argument was documented as advisory for a long time and
    this is why — yosemite's sync `Session::connect` takes no timeout and
    blocks until the router resolves the stream, so nothing at this layer
    could enforce one.

2.4 [assumed] `Session::connect`/`accept`/`forward` all take `&mut self` and
    block. A single Session shared between the dial path and an accept path
    therefore serializes. clove's SAM backend currently implements only the
    outbound + naming paths on the shared Session (behind a Mutex); the
    inbound topology is deferred (see 2.5).

2.5 [assumed] Inbound streams use STREAM FORWARD, not STREAM ACCEPT. ACCEPT
    is one accept per inbound stream on &mut self and serializes every
    inbound peer on the one session; FORWARD has the router push each inbound
    stream as a fresh TCP connection to a loopback listener we own (an
    allowed Layer-1 IP socket), so inbound concurrency is a plain accept loop,
    not a lock. This matches the "one PRIMARY session + forwarded listener"
    topology in SCOPE §4. Implemented in i2pnet::sam (SamListener /
    ForwardedStream).
    With SILENT=false (yosemite's default, set explicitly) the router prepends
    each forwarded connection with the peer's base64 destination line,
    optionally followed by FROM_PORT/TO_PORT params, terminated by '\n'; we
    read up to that newline one byte at a time (so the peer's stream payload
    is left untouched) and derive the DestHash via §1.3. The split/parse logic
    is unit-tested over loopback TCP; the exact forwarded framing and that a
    forwarded peer's derived hash reconciles with its dialed hash are the
    first things confirmed against a live router.

2.6 [open] R2 — i2pd SAM behavior under many concurrent streams on one
    session is the suspected root of XD-style flakiness. The reconnect
    supervisor (i2pnet::supervisor) is built and unit-tested against a fake
    factory; its behavior against a *real* router mid-restart, and the
    concurrency ceiling of one session, need a router to observe. First live
    observations against i2pd:

    2.6a [decided, superseded by §2.12] Outbound dial *initiation* used to
      serialize per session: yosemite's sync `connect` takes `&mut self` and
      blocks until the router resolves the stream, so `SamSession::dial` held
      the session mutex for the whole connect and one session initiated peers
      one at a time. Established streams always ran concurrently; only setup
      was serial. Dials no longer go through yosemite and take no session lock
      at all, so the engine may initiate peers concurrently.

      Kept because deleting the constraint did not delete the code shaped
      around it.
      `clove_core::swarm::sweep` went on dialling strictly one peer at a time,
      its comment still citing this entry, until a live run noticed. The cost
      was not subtle — fifty tracker peers, a 120s per-dial timeout and a
      session lifetime under two minutes meant *one* dial attempt per session,
      usually cut off in flight, giving `0 peers / 50 known` run after run from
      a sweep working exactly as written. **A superseded constraint needs its
      dependants found, not just its entry annotated.**

      The measurement, i2pd 2.61.0, host-installed, both sessions on the one
      router:

          N     dialed   connect p50   connect spread
          16      3/16         24 ms         6–28 ms
          32     30/32        419 ms       418–423 ms
          64     11/64        598 ms       597–601 ms
          128    31/128       596 ms       589–605 ms

      Thirty concurrent dials landing inside a 5 ms band is not a queue: a
      serialized one would fan out across N×d. Sub-second and flat from 32 to
      128 also says the SAM control plane does not degrade over that range —
      the R2 question — which makes control-plane collapse an unlikely
      explanation for XD-style flakiness and leaves §2.12 the better candidate.

      One caveat. The `dialed` column above is reconstructed from runs that
      only reported a connect when the *echo* also completed, so those
      distributions describe survivors and could hide slow dials entirely.
      Note the non-monotonic success rate, which is what noise looks like: one
      run per level cannot distinguish a curve from it.

    2.6e [decided] **R2 is answered in the negative: one SAM session's dial
      path does not degrade with concurrency, at least to N=200 on i2pd
      2.61.0.** Two sweeps, 2026-07-28, host-installed i2pd, both sessions on
      the one router — the first with another I2P torrent client running at
      full load, the second on a quiet machine.

      **30 runs produced a result, and every one dialed N of N.** About 1,580
      streams from N=1 to N=200; not one dial failed. Connect latency by level,
      p50 range across runs:

          N=1     332–2149 ms        N=64     437–2238 ms
          N=16    237–2806 ms        N=128    254–4827 ms
          N=32    265–5209 ms        N=200         384 ms

      The ranges overlap completely and do not order by N. An N=128 run
      connected in 254 ms, faster than every one of the six N=1 runs (332 ms at
      best); the slowest run of either sweep (5209 ms) was N=32. Within any
      single run, p99 sits within ~10 ms of p50 across as many as 200 dials. So
      dial latency is set by the router's tunnel state when the run starts, not
      by how many streams are in flight — which is what a control plane that is
      not degrading looks like.

      The contended sweep and the quiet one are indistinguishable in both
      connect and echo timings. Competing local load was not the variable; the
      per-run tunnel build was.

      **This makes SAM-under-concurrency an unlikely explanation for XD-style
      flakiness**, and leaves §2.12 (the shared session controller poisoning
      itself on an unexpected stream reply) the better candidate. R2 in
      SCOPE §7 can close.

      The echo half of those runs did not survive as cleanly, but that is
      throughput and session stability rather than the dial path, and the two
      are worth keeping apart because only one of them was ever R2's
      question.

    2.6b [assumed] A freshly created transient destination is briefly
      unreachable: a dial to it returns STREAM STATUS RESULT=CANT_REACH_PEER
      (surfaced as Protocol(Router(CantReachPeer))) for the ~seconds it takes
      the destination to build tunnels and publish its leaseSet. This is normal
      warmup, not a fault; a dial must retry through it, as the real
      peer-acquisition path does. Distinct from a *cold router*
      (reseed incomplete → no peers → CantReachPeer indefinitely), which is an
      operator/setup problem.

      Observed 2026-07-26, warmup far longer than "~seconds": both i2pd and
      Java I2P failed the same single dial, each young and firewalled at the
      time (i2pd: 15 min uptime, 33% tunnel build success, LeaseSets 0).
      Whether the floor is minutes on a *healthy* router, or those routers were
      simply not ready, is unresolved. Re-measure on warm routers before
      treating any number here as real.

    2.6g [decided] SAM `NAMING LOOKUP` of a `.b32.i2p` label is **not** a test of
      leaseSet publication. Asked to resolve a freshly created local destination,
      the routers disagreed: i2pd answered `InvalidKey`, Java I2P
      `KeyNotFound` after 3.6s, and a third returned `KeyNotFound` in 0.0s — a
      lookup that returns in no time did not consult the network. The SAM spec defines
      NAMING LOOKUP over address-book names and `ME`; b32 support is
      router-dependent and at least one router rejects the form outright.

      The wrong conclusion to draw is "the leaseSet is not resolvable, chase
      publication". Report the router's answer as fact, and look up the
      *dialer's own* destination as a control: both failing means the router
      does not answer b32 lookups for its own session destinations and the
      result says nothing about the target; only divergence between the two
      implicates publication.

2.7 [decided, closed] yosemite 0.7.0 sets no read or write timeout on its SAM
    control socket, so against a bridge that accepts the connection and then
    says nothing, `Session::new` blocks forever. It does not error, so nothing
    above it can back off, retry or log — the daemon simply sits in
    "connecting" until someone restarts it, which is the failure mode SCOPE §4
    exists to prevent. Measured in-tree against a fake bridge: of six
    misbehaviours (close, silence, garbage, endless bytes, refuse-hello,
    one-byte-dribble), four hung indefinitely.

    clove first answered this with a pre-flight probe, which left a gap the
    probe could not see past: a bridge that answered `HELLO` correctly and
    *then* stalled on `SESSION CREATE` still hung inside yosemite. Both halves
    are closed now, and the probe is gone with them — clove speaks `HELLO
    VERSION` and `SESSION CREATE` itself on one socket it owns, bounded by
    `SamConfig::probe_timeout` and `SamConfig::session_timeout` (§2.13). There
    is nothing left to pre-flight *for*: the connection the handshake opens is
    the one the session then uses. Tracked by the test
    `a_bridge_that_passes_hello_and_then_stalls_no_longer_hangs`.

2.7a [decided, closed by §2.12 and §2.14] A dialled peer that went silent
    parked its reader thread and leaked a socket for the life of the process:
    `yosemite::Stream` exposed no read or write timeout, so nothing could
    bound it. Measurable as a socket count against the bridge that climbed all
    afternoon. clove dropped such a peer from its table on the idle timeout,
    so its slot and its blocks came back — but the thread did not.

    Closed in two steps, and the first was not enough on its own:

      - §2.12: outbound streams are sockets clove owns, so
        `I2pStream::set_timeouts` bounds them and dropping one closes it.
      - §2.14: *except* when it has been split in two, which the engine does
        to every peer. Both halves are one descriptor, so dropping one closes
        nothing; the writer closes the connection explicitly now.

    Inbound was never affected: a forwarded connection is a loopback TCP
    socket clove owns, which is what lets the demux bound a connection that
    handshakes too slowly or not at all.

2.9 [decided] SAM session ids are per-router and are not necessarily released
    when the control socket closes. At least one router holds one long enough
    that a process starting seconds after a clean exit is refused with
    `DuplicateId` — observed as three consecutive runs failing at session
    setup, seconds after the previous one had exited normally.

    clove now derives every session id from a base name plus its pid and a
    sub-second component (`i2pnet::sam::unique_nickname`). The id is not the
    identity — that is the destination key (Q4) — so it can vary per process
    freely, and it stays readable enough to find in a router console.

    This is not a test-only concern. The SCOPE §4 reconnect discipline has
    cloved rebuild its session tree after losing the router; with a fixed id,
    that rebuild can be refused by the stale session it is replacing, and the
    supervisor backs off and retries into the same refusal indefinitely — the
    precise failure it exists to prevent. cloved uses a unique id per attempt
    for that reason.

2.10 [open] A SAM bridge answering `HELLO` does not mean the router can carry
    a stream. Java I2P 2.12.0 accepted the TCP connection and completed the
    pre-flight handshake (§2.7) ~25s after container start, then reset the
    connection on `SESSION CREATE`:

      seeder SAM session: Os { code: 104, kind: ConnectionReset }

    The bridge binds early in the router's startup; the router behind it was
    still coming up. Port-open and HELLO-OK are both necessary and neither is
    sufficient: before trusting a router, make it carry one stream between
    two of its own destinations. How long Java I2P actually needs before
    SESSION CREATE succeeds is still unmeasured.


2.11 [decided] The loopback topology every tier-2 test uses is *harder* on the
    router than the swarm path it stands in for, and it was gating the swarm
    path. Recorded because three live sessions were spent on the strict side of
    that asymmetry without any of them saying anything about clove.

    What the loopback test needs, and the swarm path does not:

      - a destination created seconds ago to have published its leaseSet, and
      - a *second session on the same router* to resolve that leaseSet.

    Both are netDb operations on the freshest possible data, and the first is
    what a young or firewalled router is worst at (2.6b).

    What a swarm peer does instead: resolve a destination that has been
    published for months by a router that wants to be found. And for the
    download half, our own destination need not be resolvable by anyone —
    I2P bundles the sender's leaseSet with the stream's opening message, so the
    far side answers without a netDb lookup. A leecher that no floodfill has
    ever heard of still completes a download.

    Consequence for testing, not for the wire: check the swarm path first, and
    treat a loopback failure alongside swarm success as a finding about the
    router rather than about clove.

    Note the direction of the remaining gap: *seeding* does need our leaseSet
    published and resolvable by a remote router, since that is how a peer
    reaches us at all. That is exactly what the `inbound-peer` milestone
    measures (clove_core::torrent::Torrent::inbound_peers), and it is a
    stronger result than the loopback test's inbound leg, because the router
    that resolved us is somebody else's and had its own reasons.

2.12 [decided] yosemite 0.7.0's session controller is **one state machine
    shared by the control connection and every stream operation**, and a
    stream failure on an unexpected path leaves it unusable for the life of
    the session. Found live 2026-07-27, from a run that spent ten minutes
    cycling between `downloading` and `waiting-for-router` with zero peers.

    Every entry point (`handshake_stream`, `create_stream`, `handle_response`)
    opens with `mem::replace(&mut self.state, SessionState::Poisoned)` and
    restores the state only on the paths it expects. So:

      - `STREAM STATUS RESULT=<error>` — **recovered.** yosemite explicitly
        resets `stream_state` to `Uninitialized` here, so an unreachable peer
        (`CANT_REACH_PEER`) costs one dial and nothing else. This is the
        common case on I2P and it is handled correctly.
      - an unparseable or unexpected reply to the stream `HELLO`
        (`InvalidMessage` / `InvalidState`), or a write/read failing partway
        through `connect` — **not recovered.** The controller stays `Poisoned`
        or mid-handshake, and every later `connect` on that session returns
        `InvalidState`. Permanently. The state is private, so nothing outside
        yosemite can reset it.

    Observed signature, repeating on a ~90–120s cycle: one operation fails
    with `protocol error: invalid message from router`, the next with
    `protocol error: invalid state`, and then the session is rebuilt and it
    starts over. `healthy()` did not catch it, because `send_command` writes a
    `PING` on the control socket without consulting the state machine — a
    wedged session answers PING perfectly happily while refusing every dial.

    **FIXED 2026-07-27 by not using yosemite for dials at all.** SAMv3 does
    not require a shared controller: a stream is its own connection. clove now
    opens its own socket to the bridge per dial, speaks `HELLO VERSION` then
    `STREAM CONNECT` on it, and the socket it is holding *is* the stream
    (`i2pnet::sam::dial_stream`). Nothing is shared, so nothing can be
    poisoned by another dial's failure. XD does the same thing and cannot have
    this bug for the same reason.

    Three other entries close with it, all of which were consequences of not
    owning the socket:

      - **§2.3** — the dial timeout is real. It now bounds the wait for
        `STREAM STATUS`, which is where a leaseSet lookup spends its time.
      - **§2.7a** — a dialled peer that goes silent no longer parks a thread
        and leaks a socket for the life of the process. An outbound stream is
        a `ForwardedStream` like an inbound one: `set_timeouts` works on it,
        and dropping it closes it. This was measurable as a socket count
        against the bridge that climbed all afternoon.
      - **§2.6a** — dial setup no longer serializes per session. The
        serialization point was yosemite's `connect(&mut self)` and the
        session mutex clove needed around it; a dial now takes no session
        lock at all, consulting it only for the nickname.

    The mitigation this replaces is deleted rather than kept: the `wedged`
    flag, its classification function and the supervisor's two-way teardown
    message are gone, because nothing can set them any more. So is
    `SamStream`, the yosemite-backed stream type, which nothing constructs.

    yosemite keeps what it is good at and what happens once: `SESSION CREATE`,
    `STREAM FORWARD`, `NAMING LOOKUP`. Its controller can still be poisoned in
    principle, but only by an operation that runs at setup, where the failure
    is a failed session bring-up and the supervisor's ordinary retry covers
    it. Worth reporting upstream regardless (R1) — the next consumer will hit
    it, and a state machine that poisons itself on an unexpected reply is a
    defect whether or not clove still depends on it.

2.13 [decided] **The SAM control connection has to be read, not just written
    to.** Found 2026-07-27, after a fourth live run reproduced the same
    ~90-second `downloading` / `waiting-for-router` cycle that §2.12 was
    supposed to have fixed — with zero peers connected against fifty known,
    every cycle, for the whole run.

    What clove had was a 30-second health probe: write `PING clove` on the
    session's control connection, read one line, and call the session healthy
    if that returned `Ok`. Three things were wrong with it, and together they
    made the failure it was supposed to detect unattributable.

      - **End-of-file read as health.** yosemite's `read_command` is
        `BufReader::read_line`, which returns `Ok(0)` at end-of-file, which
        yosemite maps to `Ok("")` and `is_ok()` calls success. A session the
        router had already destroyed therefore reported itself healthy for up
        to two further rounds. The observed cadence follows exactly: the probe
        that kills or witnesses the session at T+30 reports fine, T+60 reports
        fine, T+90 finally fails on a write — and the log says
        `waiting-for-router` at 105s, 211s, 316s, 437s.
      - **The reply was never examined.** Not the result, not the text,
        nothing. A `SESSION STATUS RESULT=I2P_ERROR MESSAGE="..."` — a router
        explaining precisely why it was ending the session — was read off the
        socket and dropped on the floor. Four live runs died on this cycle and
        not one produced a reason, because the one place a reason is ever
        spoken was being read into a variable nobody looked at.
      - **Nothing answered a router-initiated `PING`.** `SAMv3.2` lets the
        router ping the client. Java I2P's `SAMv3Handler` does it on every read
        timeout and answers an unanswered ping with `SESSION_ERROR "PONG
        timeout"`, ending the session. Since the control connection was only
        ever read *inside* the probe — one line per thirty seconds, treated as
        the probe's own reply — clove could not have answered one. That alone
        is fatal on Java I2P and is a plausible reason clove has never had a
        Java I2P run reach anything (§2.10).

    A dead end worth recording, so nobody spends the session on it again: i2pd
    gained SAM `PING`/`PONG` only in **2.58.0** (verified against the 2.44.0,
    2.50.2, 2.53.1, 2.56.0, 2.57.0 and 2.58.0 sources). On anything older,
    `HandleMessage` falls through to `Terminate("SAM: unexpected message")`,
    and for a socket of type `eSAMSocketTypeSession` that is
    `CloseSession(m_ID)` — so the health probe would have destroyed the very
    session it was checking, on a 30-second timer. The arithmetic fits the
    observed cadence perfectly. It is **not** what happened here: the router
    under test was 2.61.0, which answers `PING` correctly. Recorded because the
    hazard is real for anyone on an older i2pd, and because a theory fitting
    four cycles to the second can still be the wrong one.

    clove now owns its control connection outright — `HELLO VERSION` and
    `SESSION CREATE` are spoken on a socket it opens, which closes §2.7's
    residual gap on the way past — and a **watchdog thread reads that
    connection for the session's whole life**:

      - it answers `PING` with `PONG <token>`, echoed verbatim;
      - it keeps the last non-ping line the router sent;
      - end-of-file or error ends the session *immediately*, with that line
        attached to the reason.

    `healthy()` is now a flag read rather than an exchange, and the supervisor
    blocks on `wait_until_lost()` instead of polling, so teardown happens the
    moment the router hangs up rather than up to 90 seconds later, and
    `cloved` logs the router's own words for it.

    **This does not explain the ~90-second cycle**; on i2pd 2.61.0 the cause is
    still unknown. What it does is make the next run say so. The lesson is
    §5.1c's again, one layer further out: three theories about this cycle have
    now been wrong (a router fault, a poisoned library state machine, an
    unsupported PING), and each cost a live session, because the diagnostic
    that would have settled it was being read and discarded rather than not
    being available.

    `NAMING LOOKUP` was the last exchange left to the SAM library, and
    `sam::naming_lookup` now speaks it too: one HELLO, one command, one reply,
    on a socket of its own. There is no SAM library in the tree.


2.13a [decided] **Reading the router's account of a dead session is not the
    same as believing it.** Found 2026-07-31, from a live report: every
    torrent, new and existing, failing to announce with

        tracker: router refused the stream to <b32>: INVALID_ID

    indefinitely, while `clove status` reported `connected` the whole time.
    Only restarting `cloved` cleared it.

    `INVALID_ID` is the `STREAM STATUS` result for *no session has this id*.
    A dial attaches to its session by id, so this is the router saying the
    session is gone — not that a peer was unreachable. Every dial, every
    announce and every inbound attach on that session was therefore doomed,
    and clove retried into it forever.

    The reason it never recovered is the shape of §2.13's fix. That work
    correctly stopped clove *discarding* the router's last words, and stored
    them — but stored them as **text for a future log line**, in
    `Life::last_line`, to be quoted whenever something else eventually
    declared the session dead. The only thing that ever did was an I/O error
    on the control connection. So a router that ends a session by **saying
    so and leaving the connection open** hit exactly the gap between the two:
    the line was read, scrubbed, filed, and then the session was handed the
    next dial as though nothing had been said. `wait_until_lost()` blocked on
    a condvar nobody would signal, the supervisor never rebuilt, and the
    "router lost" line the watchdog exists to print was never reached.

    Two changes, because the two cases are genuinely different:

      - **The watchdog acts on what it reads.** A `SESSION STATUS` after
        setup is the router reporting on the session itself; a non-OK one
        ends it, with the router's own words attached, exactly as an
        end-of-file does. A `SESSION STATUS` carrying no `RESULT=` is treated
        as malformed rather than fatal — it is not evidence, and the dial
        path below will catch a session that is genuinely gone anyway.
      - **A dial may condemn its own session.** `INVALID_ID` is separated
        from every other refusal at the point it is classified, so it cannot
        be mistaken for `CANT_REACH_PEER` and friends, and it ends the
        session. This is the backstop for a router that destroys a session
        **silently** — no line, no close — where the refused dial is the only
        evidence that exists anywhere. Every other result stays "this peer,
        this time": one unreachable peer must never tear down a working
        session, which is the opposite failure and the worse one.

    Either path now leaves the supervisor to do what it was always written to
    do — detach, tear down, rebuild on the backoff — so the visible symptom
    becomes a `waiting-for-router` pause and a reconnect instead of a daemon
    that must be restarted by hand.

    **This is not the ~90-second cycle of §2.12/§2.13, and does not close
    it.** That was a session rebuilt too often; this is one never rebuilt at
    all. They are opposite failures and the cycle remains unexplained. What
    is worth carrying forward is narrower and applies to both: a diagnostic
    that is *recorded* but not *acted on* is one the code cannot use. §2.13
    made the router's words visible to an operator reading a log; it did not
    make them visible to clove, and that distinction cost this outage.


2.14 [decided] **A split stream is not closed by dropping one half**, and the
    engine's peer model splits every stream. Found 2026-07-27 while removing
    the last of §2.7a.

    Q5's model is a reader thread and a writer thread per peer, from
    `I2pStream::split`. For the SAM backend that is `TcpStream::try_clone`:
    two handles, one descriptor, one router-side stream. Dropping a peer from
    the engine's table drops its outgoing queue, which ends the writer, which
    drops *its* handle — and the connection stays open, because the reader
    still holds the other one. The reader then sits in a blocking read until
    the remote hangs up, which a silent peer by definition does not do.

    So every peer clove dropped — paused, idle-timed-out, or disconnected on
    session teardown, and the session tree was rebuilding every ~90 seconds —
    leaked a thread, a descriptor and a router-side stream for the life of the
    process. `Torrent::disconnect_all` carried a comment admitting it and
    deferring the fix to "the R5 keep-alive/read-timeout work", which is the
    wrong instrument: a timeout would eventually reclaim the thread, but only
    after waiting out an idle period on a connection already known to be dead.

    The fix is that the writer **closes the connection in both directions**
    when it stops (`i2pnet::I2pClose`). Both threads then end promptly, the
    descriptor is returned, and the router frees the stream. Shutting down only
    the write side would not do it: the reader is waiting on the read side.

    Recorded as its own entry rather than folded into §2.7a because the
    reasoning generalises past this bug — an abstraction that hands out two
    owning halves of one resource has to say who closes it, and `I2pStream`
    did not. Pinned by `disconnect_all_empties_the_peer_table`, which now
    asserts thread counts and that the *remote* observes the disconnect;
    without the close it fails.


2.15 [decided] **A client whose destination changes cannot be reached**, and
    clove's changed on every session rebuild.

    Q4 has always specified one persisted keypair per client, and the config
    key (`ephemeral`) and its manual page were written long ago — but the
    daemon passed `persistent_key: None` unconditionally, waiting on "key
    export confirmed against a live router". §5.1c confirmed it in the course
    of fixing something else: SAM's `SESSION STATUS DESTINATION=` field *is*
    the private key blob, so the export needed is simply keeping what the
    router already hands back on session creation.

    The cost of not having it compounds with everything above. A tracker gives
    our destination to other clients, PEX propagates it, and peers that reached
    us hold it — all pointing at an identity that stopped existing when the
    session was rebuilt. Every rebuild announced a *new* peer and abandoned the
    old one, so a run seeded the swarm with dead destinations at the rate the
    session tree cycled, and the `inbound-peer` milestone was unreachable by
    construction rather than for any of the reasons §2.11 discusses.

    clove now writes `<data_dir>/destination.key` (mode 0600, atomically, after
    the router has accepted a session with it) and replays it on every later
    `SESSION CREATE`. A file that is present but not a usable key is refused
    rather than sent to the router: `SESSION CREATE` with a malformed
    `DESTINATION=` is refused, and the supervisor would retry into the same
    refusal forever, which is §2.9's failure mode reached by a truncated file.
    The check that matters is the length one — a bare *destination* is
    well-formed, decodes cleanly, and is useless as a `DESTINATION=`, because
    it is the public half and a router cannot sign with it.


3. Reconnect discipline (i2pnet::supervisor)
--------------------------------------------

3.1 [decided] On session loss the whole session tree is rebuilt on an
    exponential backoff (default 1s doubling to 60s) with jitter (default
    0.2) to avoid a thundering-herd reconnect across many torrents. Torrents
    see a visible "waiting for router" state, never a hard failure.

3.2 [decided] A healthy session that dies once retries promptly: only failed
    *rebuilds* grow the backoff counter, not the loss itself. This keeps a
    one-off router blip from imposing a long stall.


4. Wire protocol — peer messages, PEX, metadata, and the picker
---------------------------------------------------------------

4.1 [decided] The peer wire protocol above the I2P stream is byte-identical
    to clearnet BEP 3 — no ports appear in any message; the peer is an I2P
    destination at the layer below. BEP 6 (fast) and BEP 10 (extended) are
    fully framed by the codec.

4.2 [decided] Block size is fixed at 16 KiB (BLOCK_LEN); requests and piece
    blocks over that are rejected. Matches i2psnark.

4.3 [decided] i2p_pex (clove-core::pex): a bencoded dict with `added` and
    `dropped` byte strings, each concatenated 32-byte destination hashes. No
    IPv6/`added6` keys. A message over MAX_PEX_PEERS (512) is rejected as
    spam.

    **Correction, 2026-07-27.** This entry previously asserted that "unlike
    clearnet ut_pex there are no `added.f` flag bytes". That is wrong: the
    I2P specification says the dict has **up to three keys — `added`,
    `added.f` and `dropped`** — and that i2p_pex is based on ut_pex, whose
    flag semantics are documented nowhere except libtorrent's source (which
    is what R4 has always said).

    No interop cost, by luck rather than judgement: `PexMessage::parse` takes
    the keys it knows and ignores the rest, so an `added.f` from i2psnark is
    dropped rather than refused. clove neither sends nor honours flags, which
    stays true until there is a reason. But the assertion was made without
    checking, sat in a file whose whole purpose is to be checkable, and was
    corrected only when somebody read the spec — the same failure as §5.1c,
    one layer up. [open] remains: what the flags mean and whether any of them
    should change behaviour.

    Whether PEX is working at all is now countable rather than inferable:
    Torrent::pex_learned counts destinations first heard of in a peer's PEX
    message, and the daemon publishes it as `pex_peers`. known_peers grows from
    three sources at once, so watching that number climb during a live run
    proved nothing about which source was responsible — which is why M3's "PEX
    acquisition observed" box sat unticked with no way to tick it honestly.

4.4 [decided] ut_metadata (BEP 9, clove-core::metadata): standard 16 KiB
    metadata pieces; a data message is a bencoded {msg_type,piece,
    total_size} header immediately followed by the raw piece bytes.
    Reassembled bytes are SHA-1-verified against the info-hash before use,
    so a lying peer cannot substitute a different torrent. `total_size`
    over 8 MiB is refused up front. Extension ids: clove advertises
    i2p_pex=1, ut_metadata=2 and replies to a peer using the ids that peer
    advertised (peers reply to us using ours). fetch_metadata drives the
    magnet bootstrap: BT+extension handshake, request all pieces, verify,
    then MetaInfo::from_info_dict. Verified in-tree over the mock (a client
    fetches an info dict from a serving instance).

4.7 [decided] **A BEP 3 request nobody answers is permanent unless you give it
    a deadline**, and two independent stalls follow from not having one. Found
    2026-07-27 from the first live run that got far enough to stall on
    something other than the session (48%, 17.6 MiB down, 39 of 82 pieces).

    The wire protocol offers no help here. There is no acknowledgement, no
    error and no obligation to answer a `request`, so a dropped one is
    indistinguishable from a slow one. Peers drop them routinely — overloaded,
    lost the piece, changed their mind — and on I2P a stream can also stay open
    while its tunnels are useless.

    **First stall: blocks nobody re-offers.** A request that was never answered
    stayed in the peer's pipeline and in the picker's in-flight set forever.
    The block was never asked for again and its piece never completed, while
    the peer's usable pipeline depth shrank by one, permanently. The live
    signature is unmistakable and was measurable straight off the milestone
    table: 43 pieces outstanding, holding 502 of the 688 blocks they needed —
    **every one of them roughly three-quarters finished, none of them
    finishing**. Throughput decayed from ~39 KiB/s over the first 166 seconds
    to ~3.4 KiB/s over the next 3400 as slots filled with debts nobody owed.

    Nothing else in the engine reclaimed them. `Choke` already drained
    in-flight blocks back to the picker (correctly), and the idle timeout
    dropped a peer that went *silent* — but a peer that keeps sending
    keep-alives and `have`s while not answering refreshes `last_seen` on every
    one, so it never looks idle. Politeness was the whole attack surface.

    **Second stall: pipelines that drain and are never refilled.** `fill_requests`
    ran on exactly two events, a block arriving and an unchoke. A peer whose
    pipeline emptied at a moment when the picker had nothing to give — because
    every remaining block was in flight with somebody else — was therefore
    never asked again: it sat connected, interested, unchoked and permanently
    idle, and the event that would have woken it is a block arriving on the
    connection that has nothing outstanding. This one needs no misbehaving peer
    at all; one slow peer holding the last few blocks is enough. It is also why
    fixing the first stall alone does not help: by the time the expired blocks
    go back to the picker, the honest peers that could take them have gone
    quiet.

    clove now stamps every in-flight block with the time it was requested and,
    on the maintenance tick, expires anything past `DEFAULT_REQUEST_TIMEOUT`
    (90s) back to the picker, tops up *every* eligible peer rather than only
    the ones that timed out, and drops a peer that lets requests expire for
    `REQUEST_STRIKES` (3) consecutive rounds while delivering nothing — its
    slot is worth more to the swarm than its connection, and the dial sweep may
    pick it up again after its retry backoff.

    **The endgame is not a substitute and hid this from every test.** Duplicate
    requests begin only within `DEFAULT_ENDGAME_BLOCKS` (32) of completion, so
    a handful of stuck blocks is quietly rescued and a small fixture can never
    show the defect — the evil-peer suite's five-block torrent is inside the
    endgame window from the first byte. Live it was ~186 blocks across ~45
    peers, far outside it. The regression test therefore builds its own
    128-piece torrent and four peers that take a full pipeline each and answer
    nothing: without the deadline it stalls at exactly 64 of 128 pieces, four
    pipelines' worth, which is the live shape in miniature.

    Worth stating as a rule, since this is the second time a test fixture has
    been too small to contain the bug it was meant to catch: a threshold that
    rescues small cases must be *exceeded* by any test that claims to exercise
    what happens without it.

4.5 [decided] Magnet links (clove-core::magnet): `magnet:?xt=urn:btih:<hex
    40 | base32 32>`; `dn` and `tr` are percent-decoded, `tr` filtered to
    I2P-only like .torrent announce URLs.

4.6 [open] I2P `maggot://` links are a distinct, underspecified format.
    Not parsed yet — we will not guess a grammar we cannot verify against
    real examples. Add support (and the grammar) here once confirmed.


5. HTTP tracker announces (clove-core::tracker)
-----------------------------------------------

5.1 [assumed] An announce is an HTTP/1.1 GET to the tracker's I2P
    destination. clove sends the standard BEP 3 query params plus:
      - `compact=1` (we require the 32-byte-hash compact form, §1.4);
      - `port=6881` as a placeholder — I2P has no port, but trackers/params
        parsers expect the key;
      - `ip=<our base64 destination>` — the I2P convention for telling the
        tracker our destination, since there is no IP to observe.

    [decided] as of 2026-07-27, and now against the normative spec rather than
    by inference. The I2P BitTorrent specification (i2p.net, "Bittorrent over
    I2P") states it plainly: *"The value of the ip key is the base 64 of the
    client's Destination"*, that *"current Destinations for clients are 387 or
    more bytes (516 or more in Base 64 encoding)"*, and that a tracker
    *"should probably decode and reject bad Base64 when announced"*.

    All three sentences describe what happened to us. The parameter is
    required (omitting it answers `failure reason: Missing key: ip`); the
    value is the 387-plus-byte Destination, not the 679-byte private key blob
    clove was sending; and postman's tracker rejecting the bad value was the
    spec's own recommended behaviour. See §5.1c. This closes the oldest
    [assumed] in this file.

    Observed variant, unconfirmed: XD (`lib/tracker/http.go`) builds `ip` from
    its network address plus a `.i2p` suffix rather than from a base64
    Destination. Whether that resolves to the same thing depends on what its
    SAM layer returns from `Addr()`, which the announce code does not say —
    recorded because a second form may exist in the wild, not as a licence to
    send one.

5.1a [decided] Trackers answer `Transfer-Encoding: chunked`, and clove's HTTP
    client did not implement it. postman's `announce.php` does, as any PHP
    script behind an ordinary webserver will. The reader fell through to its
    read-until-close branch and handed the *chunk framing* to the bencode
    parser, which reported "not bencode" — accurate about `1a4\r\n…` and
    useless. Every announce failed identically, so the client acquired no
    peers at all, on any torrent, ever (found live 2026-07-27).

    Decoding is implemented in `clove_core::http` with the same hostile-input
    discipline as the rest: bounded size lines, extensions parsed off and
    ignored, `max_body` checked against the announced chunk size before
    anything is allocated, trailers consumed rather than mistaken for body,
    and chunked overriding a `Content-Length` sent alongside it (RFC 9112
    §6.1). Announces that are *not* bencode now carry a bounded printable
    prefix of what did arrive, so the next surprise is one log line rather
    than a session.

5.1b [decided] `tracker2.postman.i2p` answered an announce with **HTTP 200 and a
    styled HTML page** (observed 2026-07-27, i2pd 2.x, live). Not a bencoded
    `failure reason`, not an error status — a web page, served where an
    announce belongs.

    **RESOLVED to a refusal, 2026-07-27.** With HTML summarised rather than
    quoted, the page names itself:

      HTML page titled "PaTracker 1.7.8 | Blocked Request"; text begins
      "Request denied! Your request was found to be in violation of the
       site's policy. In most cases this is caused by issues related to I2P,
       like incomplete data and such."

    And the endpoint is demonstrably alive: the same URL fetched from a
    browser through the router's SOCKS proxy answers
    `d14:failure reason22:Missing key: info_hashe` — correct bencode for a
    bare GET. So `/announce.php` routes, PaTracker is running, and it is
    **clove's announce specifically** that it refuses.

    That narrows it to the request. The candidates, in the order they are
    worth deleting:
      1. `ip=<base64 destination>` (§5.1, still [assumed]). The tracker sees
         our destination from the stream itself; a client asserting one in a
         parameter is the most likely thing a policy check objects to, and
         "incomplete data related to I2P" reads like a complaint about
         exactly this field.
      2. `port=6881`, a placeholder for a protocol with no ports.
      3. `numwant`, `compact`, `event` — ordinary, but untested here.

    Do not reason about which; delete them one at a time. The daemon now logs
    the literal URL it sent on every failed announce, so the test is to paste
    that into the same browser and remove parameters until it stops being
    refused. One round of that is worth more than another round of theory —
    three prior theories about this failure (address book, chunked encoding,
    wrong endpoint) were all wrong, and each cost a live session.

    **It is postman.** The page's own stylesheet declares `--postman:` and
    embeds an SVG of a postman; 14151 bytes, `content-type text/html;
    charset=utf-8`. So the destination is right and the address book is not
    the problem — that candidate is closed. What remains:
      (a) postman's webserver declines the request and serves the site
          instead. Two shapes in clove's announce were unusual enough to be
          worth removing before blaming the endpoint: `Content-Length: 0` on
          a bodyless GET, which almost nothing on the web sends, and **no
          `User-Agent` at all**. Both are fixed; §5.1's `ip=` form remains
          [assumed] and is the next candidate if they were not it.
      (b) `/announce.php` is no longer the announce endpoint, and the server
          answers 200 with its front page for paths it does not route.

    Settle it from outside clove before changing anything else — one request
    through the router's HTTP proxy says which:

        curl -x http://127.0.0.1:4444 -i http://tracker2.postman.i2p/announce.php

    A bencoded `failure reason` means the endpoint is live and clove's request
    is at fault; the same HTML page means the endpoint is (b).

    Two previews were spent learning that a modern page's first 512 bytes are
    its stylesheet. `preview` now summarises HTML — `<title>` plus the first
    visible words — rather than quoting the head, which is what "identify the
    page" actually needs.

5.1c [decided] **SAM's `DESTINATION=` field is the session's private key
    blob, not its destination — and clove was announcing the whole thing.**
    Root cause of every failure in §5.1b, found 2026-07-27 by decoding the
    announce clove actually sent.

    `SESSION STATUS RESULT=OK DESTINATION=<b64>` returns the *private keys*.
    The public destination is only their first 387-or-so bytes; the remainder
    is the private crypto and signing key. Captured from i2pd: 908 base64
    characters, 679 bytes — 391 of destination (a KEY certificate with a
    4-byte payload) and 288 of key material. Both halves are one unbroken
    base64 run, so nothing about the string says where one ends.

    Consequences, all of which had been misread as something else:

      - **Every announce carried our private keys** to the tracker in `ip=`.
        postman's PaTracker refused each one as a policy violation, which was
        correct and generous. The bisect that found it: dropping `ip=`
        entirely changes the answer to `failure reason: Missing key: ip`, so
        the parameter is required *and* ours was wrong.
      - **Our own `DestHash` was the hash of the private blob.** Every b32
        clove printed, published over PEX, or dialled in a loopback test named
        nothing at all. A router asked to resolve it can only fail — which is
        precisely the "lease set not found" that was recorded at the time as a
        router defect, and the `CantReachPeer` that §2.6b blamed on young
        routers. Those observations stood; their attribution was wrong.

    clove now cuts the blob to its destination before using it, at the one
    place it enters the process (`i2pnet::addr::destination_bytes`). The
    length comes from the certificate's own header, so it survives whatever
    key types a router uses. Anything too short to hold a certificate header
    is refused rather than guessed at: a wrong answer here is a wrong
    identity, and a wrong identity fails silently everywhere at once.

    The lesson for the rest of this file: **entries written from live router
    logs were still wrong**, because the evidence was consistent with a router
    bug and nobody decoded what we were sending. Prefer decoding your own
    output before attributing a fault upstream.

5.6 [decided] **`completed` is an event, and clove was treating it as a
    periodic report.** Found 2026-07-28 in the first live run that completed a
    download (i2pd 2.61.0, 20.4 MiB in 227s) and then served nothing at all
    across a 900-second seeding window.

    `announces_ok 1` for the whole run says it: one announce, sent at 76s while
    still a leecher with `left` equal to the entire file, and nothing after it.
    The `AnnounceState` machine was right — `next_event(complete)` returns
    `Completed` and reports it once — but `announce_loop` only ever announced
    when the tracker's interval said so, and postman's is half an hour. So the
    completion announce was scheduled for well after the run ended, and for the
    whole seeding window the tracker went on handing our destination to peers
    as a leecher that held nothing. Nobody had any reason to ask us for a byte.

    BEP 3 asks for `completed` at the moment the download finishes, and every
    client sends it there. It belongs with `started` (the first announce, which
    no interval governs) and `stopped` (which clove already sends on teardown
    with no regard for one) — events, not cadence.

    The fix is deliberately *not* `AnnounceState::make_due`, whose documented
    contract is that nothing automatic may use it, because that rule is a good
    one and worth keeping intact. `completion_due` is a separate, much narrower
    door: it does nothing before the first announce and nothing once the
    completion announce has gone out, so it can bring forward exactly one
    announce in a torrent's lifetime and cannot become a way to hammer a
    tracker. The announcer watches for the torrent finishing and opens it.

    Found alongside, and fixed with it: a torrent that was *already* complete
    when its announcer started would report `Completed` on its second announce.
    BEP 3 says not to — the client had the file before it announced at all, so
    there is no download to report — and a tracker counts a snatch every time
    it sees one. clove rebuilds its announcers on every session blip, so this
    was one phantom snatch per restart, per seeding torrent.
    `AnnounceState::already_complete` is the constructor for that case.

5.2 [decided] Any query already present in the announce URL (e.g. postman's
    `/announce.php?...`) is preserved; clove appends its params with the
    correct `?`/`&` separator.

5.3 [decided] Announce timing: honor the tracker's `interval`, floored at a
    local 60s minimum so a misconfigured tracker cannot make us hammer.
    On failure, exponential backoff from 30s capped at 30 min. A tracker
    `failure reason` is surfaced to the operator, not retried as if
    transient.

5.4 [open] Whether i2psnark/opentracker expect `event=started` on the very
    first announce only, and any tracker-specific quirks (numwant caps,
    `&supportcrypto`, etc.), to be confirmed live at M3 and recorded here.

5.5 [decided] A tracker URL's host is usually an **address-book name**
    (`tracker2.postman.i2p`), not a b32, and the two resolve through entirely
    different machinery: the address book is a local file the router fills from
    HTTP subscriptions, while a b32 resolves through the netDb. A router that
    has not completed its subscription fetch cannot resolve the first form at
    all, however healthy it otherwise is.

    This interacts badly with the negative-lookup backoff (R6,
    `i2pnet::naming`): the hold doubles from 30s to 30 minutes, so a name that
    is unknown at startup is retried *less* often as time passes. The observable
    symptom is therefore not a stream of failed lookups but an absence of
    lookups, which reads from outside like a stalled client.

    Neither behaviour is wrong — caching a miss is the point — but both were
    invisible. The lookup error now states the remaining hold, and
    `try_fetch_round` reports which stage failed rather than dropping it
    (first found live 2026-07-27). An operator hitting
    this can sidestep it with a b32 tracker URL.

    **Confirmed live, 2026-07-28**, on a router whose address-book
    subscription had never been fetched. Its SAM session came up normally at
    61s and every announce then failed with `router error: KeyNotFound` for
    `tracker2.postman.i2p`, followed by the negative-cache hold announcing
    itself:

      resolving tracker tracker2.postman.i2p: protocol error:
        `router error: KeyNotFound`
      … negative-cached after 2 failed lookup(s); not asking the router
        again for 29s

    Nothing here implicates clove, and — the point of §5.5 — it took one
    glance to say so, where the same failure a week earlier presented as a
    client that sat silently doing nothing. The router's word (`KeyNotFound`,
    not `InvalidKey`, not a timeout) also distinguishes an empty address book
    from a netDb lookup that failed, which is the difference between "wait for
    the subscription" and "the destination is gone".
